\title{Fermat's Little Theorem at $p=5$: A Unified Mechanism\\ and a Sharp Divisibility Strengthening}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{July 2, 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
We study the elementary but instructive fact that $a^5 - a$ is divisible by $5$ for every integer $a$. Rather than treating the modulus $5$ as a special constant to be dispatched by residue-class case analysis, we derive the result from the general integer form of \emph{Fermat's Little Theorem}: for every prime $p$ and every integer $a$, $p \mid a^p - a$. The engine is the field identity $x^p = x$ valid on the ring of integers modulo a prime $p$, transported back to $\mathbb{Z}$ through the standard bridge between ``divisibility by $p$'' and ``vanishing modulo $p$.'' Specializing to $p = 5$ recovers the headline claim as a one-line corollary. We then prove a genuine strengthening: $30 \mid a^5 - a$ for all integers $a$, obtained by combining divisibility by the pairwise-coprime primes $2$, $3$, and $5$. We record the elementary factorisation $a^5 - a = a(a-1)(a+1)(a^2+1)$ as a complementary window on the same phenomenon, and we develop two consequences: the congruence $a^5 \equiv a \pmod 5$ and the summed divisibility $5 \mid \sum_{k<n}(k^5 - k)$. We close by conjecturing the general shape of the universal divisor $M(n)$ of $a^n - a$ and relating it to Korselt's criterion and Carmichael numbers.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

Among the first nontrivial divisibility facts a student of number theory meets is the claim that $a^5 - a$ is always a multiple of $5$. It is easy to verify on examples:
\[
2^5 - 2 = 30, \qquad 3^5 - 3 = 240, \qquad 7^5 - 7 = 16800,
\]
and each of these is divisible by $5$. Two natural pedagogical routes exist. The first is a direct case analysis on the residue of $a$ modulo $5$; the second, which we adopt, is to recognize the statement as the instance $p = 5$ of a single general mechanism---\emph{Fermat's Little Theorem}---and to derive it uniformly.

The advantages of the general route are twofold. First, it is conceptually economical: one proves a theorem about \emph{all} primes and reads off $p = 5$ for free. Second, and more interestingly, the same machinery reveals that the true answer to ``by what is $a^5 - a$ always divisible?'' is not $5$ but $30$. This threefold sharpening---a phenomenon of the problem \emph{giving more than was asked}---is the mathematical heart of this note.

\section{Preliminaries and notation}

Throughout, $a \in \mathbb{Z}$ denotes an arbitrary integer and $p$ a prime. We write $m \mid n$ for ``$m$ divides $n$'' and $a \equiv b \pmod m$ for ``$m \mid a - b$.''

For a positive integer $m$, the \emph{ring of integers modulo $m$}, denoted $\mathbb{Z}/m\mathbb{Z}$, consists of the residue classes $\{0, 1, \dots, m-1\}$ under addition and multiplication carried out and then reduced modulo $m$.

\begin{fact}[Prime moduli give fields]\label{fact:field}
If $p$ is prime, then $\mathbb{Z}/p\mathbb{Z}$ is a field: every nonzero residue class has a multiplicative inverse.
\end{fact}

\begin{lemma}[Bridge principle]\label{lem:bridge}
For every integer $m$ and every prime $p$, we have $p \mid m$ if and only if the residue class of $m$ in $\mathbb{Z}/p\mathbb{Z}$ is zero.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
By definition the residue class of $m$ is the remainder of $m$ upon division by $p$, and this remainder is $0$ exactly when $p \mid m$.
\end{proof}

Lemma~\ref{lem:bridge} is the load-bearing device of the whole development: it lets us prove a divisibility statement in $\mathbb{Z}$ by instead proving that an expression vanishes in $\mathbb{Z}/p\mathbb{Z}$, where the field structure of Fact~\ref{fact:field} is available.

\section{The general mechanism and the case $p=5$}

\begin{theorem}[Power identity on a prime clock]\label{thm:power}
Let $p$ be prime. Then in $\mathbb{Z}/p\mathbb{Z}$ we have $x^p = x$ for every element $x$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
If $x = 0$ the identity is trivial. If $x \neq 0$, then $x$ lies in the multiplicative group $(\mathbb{Z}/p\mathbb{Z})^\times$, which has order $p - 1$. By Lagrange's theorem the order of $x$ divides $p - 1$, so $x^{p-1} = 1$; multiplying by $x$ gives $x^p = x$. Equivalently, one may use the ``freshman's dream'' $(x+1)^p = x^p + 1$, which follows because $p \mid \binom{p}{k}$ for $0 < k < p$, and induct on $x$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Integer form of Fermat's Little Theorem]\label{thm:flt}
For every prime $p$ and every integer $a$,
\[
p \mid a^p - a.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Reduce modulo $p$. In $\mathbb{Z}/p\mathbb{Z}$, the residue class of $a^p - a$ equals $x^p - x$ where $x$ is the class of $a$. By Theorem~\ref{thm:power}, $x^p = x$, so $x^p - x = 0$; that is, the class of $a^p - a$ is zero. By Lemma~\ref{lem:bridge}, $p \mid a^p - a$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Fermat's Little Theorem for $p=5$]\label{cor:five}
For every integer $a$, $\quad 5 \mid a^5 - a.$
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:flt} with the prime $p = 5$.
\end{proof}

\section{An elementary factorisation}

\begin{theorem}[Factorisation of $a^5 - a$]\label{thm:factor}
For every integer $a$,
\[
a^5 - a = a\,(a-1)\,(a+1)\,(a^2 + 1).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Expand: $a(a-1)(a+1) = a(a^2 - 1) = a^3 - a$, and then $(a^3 - a)(a^2 + 1) = a^5 + a^3 - a^3 - a = a^5 - a$.
\end{proof}

This factorisation exhibits $a^5 - a$ as $a$ times its two neighbours $a - 1$ and $a + 1$ (three consecutive integers) times the quadratic $a^2 + 1$. Among any three consecutive integers, one is divisible by $3$ and at least one is even, so $6 \mid a(a-1)(a+1)$ with no further work.

\section{The sharp strengthening: divisibility by $30$}

\begin{lemma}[Small-prime divisibility]\label{lem:small}
For every integer $a$, each of $2$, $3$, and $5$ divides $a^5 - a$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
For $p = 5$ this is Corollary~\ref{cor:five}. For $p = 2$ and $p = 3$, note from Theorem~\ref{thm:factor} that $a(a-1)(a+1)$ divides $a^5 - a$; among three consecutive integers one is even and one is a multiple of $3$, so $2$ and $3$ both divide the product and hence $a^5 - a$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Coprime divisors multiply]\label{lem:coprime}
If $d_1, \dots, d_k$ are pairwise coprime positive integers each dividing an integer $N$, then their product $d_1 \cdots d_k$ divides $N$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Induct on $k$. The case $k = 1$ is trivial. For the step, suppose $D = d_1 \cdots d_{k-1} \mid N$ and $d_k \mid N$ with $\gcd(D, d_k) = 1$. Write $N = Dq$. Since $d_k \mid Dq$ and $\gcd(d_k, D) = 1$, Euclid's lemma gives $d_k \mid q$, whence $D d_k \mid Dq = N$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Sharp divisibility]\label{thm:thirty}
For every integer $a$, $\quad 30 \mid a^5 - a.$
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{lem:small} the pairwise-coprime numbers $2, 3, 5$ each divide $a^5 - a$. By Lemma~\ref{lem:coprime} their product $2 \cdot 3 \cdot 5 = 30$ divides $a^5 - a$.
\end{proof}

That $30$ is optimal is witnessed at $a = 2$: $2^5 - 2 = 30$, so no integer larger than $30$ can divide $a^5 - a$ for all $a$. Thus $\gcd_{a \in \mathbb{Z}}(a^5 - a) = 30$.

\section{Consequences}

\begin{theorem}[Congruence form]\label{thm:cong}
For every integer $a$, $\quad a^5 \equiv a \pmod 5$, equivalently $a^5 \bmod 5 = a \bmod 5$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Corollary~\ref{cor:five}, $5 \mid a^5 - a$, which is the definition of $a^5 \equiv a \pmod 5$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Summed form]\label{thm:sum}
For every natural number $n$, $\quad 5 \mid \sum_{k=0}^{n-1}(k^5 - k).$
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Corollary~\ref{cor:five}, each summand $k^5 - k$ is divisible by $5$; a finite sum of multiples of $5$ is again a multiple of $5$.
\end{proof}

\section{Applications}

\textbf{Fast modular exponentiation.} Theorem~\ref{thm:cong} collapses fifth powers modulo $5$ instantly. More generally, Theorem~\ref{thm:flt} underlies $a^p \equiv a \pmod p$ and, via $a^{p-1} \equiv 1 \pmod p$, the computation of modular inverses.

\textbf{Primality testing.} Fermat's Little Theorem is the basis of the Fermat primality test; Theorem~\ref{thm:thirty} and its generalizations describe the composite moduli that fool it.

\textbf{Checksum schemes.} Congruences $a^k \equiv a$ underlie error-detecting checksums where raising to a power must not disturb a residue.

\section{Discussion and future directions}

\textbf{The universal divisor is squarefree.} For $n \geq 2$, let $M(n)$ be the largest integer dividing $a^n - a$ for every $a$. We conjecture
\[
M(n) = \prod_{\substack{p \text{ prime}\\ (p-1)\mid(n-1)}} p,
\]
always squarefree. For $n = 5$, $(p-1)\mid 4$ selects $p \in \{2,3,5\}$, giving $M(5) = 30$.

\textbf{Sharpness at $a = 2$.} We conjecture the extremal witness realizing $M(n)$ can always be taken as $a = 2$; for $n = 5$, $2^5 - 2 = 30 = M(5)$.

\textbf{Carmichael-type converse.} A composite $m$ satisfies $a^n \equiv a \pmod m$ for all $a$ iff $m$ is squarefree and every prime factor $p$ satisfies $(p-1)\mid(n-1)$. Korselt's criterion is the case $n - 1 = m - 1$; the result $30 \mid a^5 - a$ is the $n = 5$ instance.

\textbf{Factorisation depth.} The factorisation of $a^n - a$ into $a$ times cyclotomic polynomials evaluated at $a$ governs the length of an elementary residue-based proof; we conjecture the relevant count is $p$ for each contributing prime $p$.

\section{Conclusion}

Starting from the request to show $5 \mid a^5 - a$, we derived it as a one-line corollary of the integer form of Fermat's Little Theorem, itself a transport of the field identity $x^p = x$ across the divisibility--vanishing bridge. The same toolkit sharpened the answer to the optimal $30 \mid a^5 - a$, and yielded a congruence and a summed-divisibility consequence at no extra cost. The elementary factorisation offered independent confirmation, and the case $p = 5$ pointed directly at a conjectural theory of universal divisors and its connection to Carmichael numbers.

